// src/bc_read_stub.pdx — user-space stub for libpdx-volume's
// `lpv_bc_read(lba, out_ptr) -> u64` extern (paideia-os#2574 / PFS-07
// mount-side adaptation).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this exists
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// libpdx-volume v1.1.7 (PFS-07) replaced `snap_chain_walk`'s v1.1.0
// 5-arg fail-closed stub with a real walker body whose per-step
// block read is routed through an EXTERNALLY provided block-read
// authority:
//
//     lpv_bc_read(lba: u64, out_ptr: u64) -> u64
//         0  on success (4096 bytes written to [out_ptr..out_ptr+4096))
//         !0 on error (walker reports LPV_SNAP_ERR_READ = 0x0B01)
//
// `lpv_bc_read` is an UNRESOLVED extern in libpdx-volume's own
// `vol_snapshot.o` -- preserving its "zero cap-invoke syscalls in the
// own call graph" discipline. The intended real-world provider is a
// thin shim over KIND_BLOCK_CACHE's BC_OP_READ cap-invoke op; the
// kernel-side cap kind + op dispatcher do not yet exist in the
// paideia-os tree (KERNEL GAP #B in the earlier `snapshot_wire.pdx`
// module header -- which this file documents as PARTIALLY resolved:
// the walker body landed, the block-cache shim did not).
//
// Without a definition, `ld -nostdlib --warn-common --fatal-warnings`
// (the mount.pdxfs link line) would refuse to produce an ELF because
// `vol_snapshot.o` references the symbol and no other object defines
// it. The link-fail cascade would then stage a 512B zero-filled
// stub `mount.pdxfs.elf` into the kernel build via `tools/build.sh`'s
// "individual tool failures are NON-FATAL" branch, hollowing out the
// whole tool rather than just the one surface.
//
// Mirrors the established `tools/user-sign-stub.pdx` pattern
// (paideia-os#1976): a user-space ELF cannot reach a Rust-runtime
// extern-C thunk, so a stub emits a sentinel that the calling tool
// routes to a STUB result_code. Here the "calling tool" is the walker
// itself; it maps `lpv_bc_read` non-zero to `LPV_SNAP_ERR_READ`
// (0x0B01), which `snapshot_wire_list` in turn maps to
// `SW_ERR_KERNEL_NOT_IMPL` (1), which `main.pdx` already emits as
// `MR_RESULT_SNAPSHOT_NOT_IMPL` regardless of the SW_* code -- so
// mount.pdxfs's observable `--snapshot-list` behaviour is unchanged
// (exit 3 + SNAPSHOT_NOT_IMPL emission) while the link line now
// succeeds.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Return-code contract
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Returns `BC_READ_ERR_NO_SHIM = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD`, chosen two below
// the sign-stub's PDXB_SIGN_STUB (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFE) and the
// verify-stub's 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF so a caller inspecting the
// sentinel can discriminate across all three families. The walker
// treats any non-zero return as `LPV_SNAP_ERR_READ`, so the exact
// value is not load-bearing for walker semantics -- it IS load-bearing
// for a future diagnostic tool that wants to tell "block-cache shim
// is a stub" from "block-cache shim ran but the block was missing".
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Replacement strategy
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// When the KIND_BLOCK_CACHE shim lands (paideia-os monorepo kernel
// side), this file is removed from `src/` and replaced by a thin
// `pdxb_bc_read.pdx` that cap-invokes BC_OP_READ. The replacement
// MUST keep the same `(u64, u64) -> u64` signature + 0-is-success
// convention; a mismatch produces a link-time error that is a louder
// signal than a runtime surprise, same discipline as
// `user-sign-stub.pdx` enforces for the signing stubs. Mount.pdxfs's
// own `snapshot_wire_list` + the walker body stay unchanged across
// that swap.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder-pitfall discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Pure leaf, no auto-prologue. One instruction + ret. No `test`;
// no `add reg,[mem]`; no multiline `pub let =`. Immediate is a full
// 64-bit value loaded via `mov r64, imm64` (paideia-as emits the
// 10-byte REX.W + MOV imm64 form). Labels: none needed.

module BcReadStub = structure {

  // Sentinel the walker observes as LPV_SNAP_ERR_READ-equivalent.
  // Deliberately distinct from PDXB_SIGN_STUB (0xFF..FE) and
  // PDXB_VER_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED (0xFF..FF).
  pub let BC_READ_ERR_NO_SHIM : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD

  // Signature mirrors libpdx-volume's own module-header declaration of
  // the extern (vol_snapshot.pdx: `lpv_bc_read(lba, out_ptr) -> u64`).
  pub let lpv_bc_read : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (lba: u64) (out_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "paideia-os#2574 (PFS-07) user-space block-cache stub. Pure leaf, no manual pushes, no inner calls -- paideia-as skips the non-leaf auto-prologue. mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD (BC_READ_ERR_NO_SHIM, 10-byte REX.W+MOV imm64); ret. Walker maps non-zero to LPV_SNAP_ERR_READ; snapshot_wire_list maps that to SW_ERR_KERNEL_NOT_IMPL; main.pdx emits MR_RESULT_SNAPSHOT_NOT_IMPL. Labels: none.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
